{"name":"Must Love Dogs","hex":"#9b6b45","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"must","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To do as a requirement; indicates that the sentence subject is required as an imperative or directive to execute the sentence predicate, with failure to do so resulting in a failure or negative consequence.","To do with certainty; indicates that the speaker is certain that the subject will have executed the predicate."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Something that is mandatory, required or recommended."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/must"},{"term":"love","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A deep caring for the existence of another.","Strong affection."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To have a strong affection for (someone or something).","To need, thrive on."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/love"},{"term":"dogs","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of dog"]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Feet, from rhyming slang dog's meat. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","Toes"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/dogs"}],"entries":[{"title":"Must Love Dogs","blocks":[{"text":"Must Love Dogs is a 2005 American romantic comedy film based on Claire Cook's 2002 novel of the same name. Starring Diane Lane and John Cusack, it is the second and final film to be written, produced, and directed by Gary David Goldberg and was produced on a budget of $30 million. The film focuses on a woman's struggle with divorce and meeting new people afterward."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/9/9f/Must_Love_Dogs.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Must_Love_Dogs","wikidata":"Q151811"},{"title":"Must","blocks":[{"text":"Must is freshly crushed fruit juice that contains the skins, seeds, and stems of the fruit. The solid portion of the must is called pomace and typically makes up 7–23% of the total weight of the must. Making must is the first step in winemaking. Because of its high glucose content, typically between 10 and 15%, must is also used as a sweetener in a variety of cuisines. Unlike commercially sold grape juice, which is filtered and pasteurized, must is thick with particulate matter, opaque, and comes in various shades of brown and purple. The name comes from the Latin vinum mustum; lit. 'young wine'."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/Mosto.jpg/330px-Mosto.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Must","wikidata":"Q278818"},{"title":"Love","blocks":[{"text":"Love encompasses a range of strong and positive emotional and mental states, from the most sublime virtue or good habit, the deepest interpersonal affection, to the simplest pleasure. An example of this range of meanings is that the love of a mother differs from the love of a spouse, which differs from the love for food. Most commonly, love refers to a feeling of strong attraction and emotional attachment."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Love","wikidata":"Q316"},{"title":"Dog","blocks":[{"text":"The dog is a domesticated descendant of wolves. Also called the domestic dog, it was selectively bred during the Late Pleistocene by hunter-gatherers. Dogs and the modern gray wolf share a common ancestor. Dogs were the first species to be domesticated over 14,000 years ago, before the development of agriculture, though genetic studies suggest the domestication process may have begun over 25,000 years ago. Due to their long association with humans, dogs have gained the ability to thrive on a starch-rich diet that would be inadequate for other canids."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7a/Huskiesatrest.jpg/330px-Huskiesatrest.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dog","wikidata":"Q144"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Aceto_Balsamico.jpg/500px-Aceto_Balsamico.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Aceto_Balsamico.jpg/1280px-Aceto_Balsamico.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Aceto_Balsamico.jpg/500px-Aceto_Balsamico.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Bottle of traditional balsamic vinegar from Modena, Italy, containing grape must","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAceto_Balsamico.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/Columpio_Veracruz_059.jpg/500px-Columpio_Veracruz_059.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/Columpio_Veracruz_059.jpg/1280px-Columpio_Veracruz_059.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/Columpio_Veracruz_059.jpg/500px-Columpio_Veracruz_059.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Fraternal love (Prehispanic sculpture from 250 to 900 CE, of Huastec origin). Museum of Anthropology in Xalapa, Veracruz, Mexico","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AColumpio_Veracruz_059.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e9/Gothaer_Liebespaar.jpg/500px-Gothaer_Liebespaar.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e9/Gothaer_Liebespaar.jpg/500px-Gothaer_Liebespaar.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Pair of Lovers. 1480–1485","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3